🧱 Section 1: Kafka Fundamentals
1. What is Apache Kafka?
Apache Kafka is a distributed, fault-tolerant, high-throughput event streaming platform. Originally built at LinkedIn, it’s designed to handle real-time data pipelines and streaming workloads at massive scale.

Think of it as a commit log that never forgets — producers append events, consumers read them at their own pace, and Kafka retains everything for a configurable duration. It decouples your services beautifully.

2. Why is Kafka used in Microservices Architecture?
In microservices, services must communicate without tight coupling. Kafka enables:

Asynchronous communication — producers don’t wait for consumers
Event-driven architecture — services react to events rather than polling
Resilience — if a consumer is down, messages wait; nothing is lost
Scalability — consumers can be scaled independently
Audit trail — every event is persisted and replayable
REST calls create temporal coupling (both services must be up). Kafka eliminates that entirely.

3. What are Topics and Partitions in Kafka?
A Topic is a named feed or category to which records are published — like a table in a database, but append-only.

A Partition is how a topic is split for parallelism. Each partition is an ordered, immutable sequence of records. Key properties:

A topic with 6 partitions can be consumed by up to 6 consumers in parallel (within a group)
Messages with the same key always go to the same partition (ordering guarantee per key)
Partitions are distributed across brokers for load balancing
Topic: orders
├── Partition 0 → Broker 1
├── Partition 1 → Broker 2
└── Partition 2 → Broker 3
4. What is an Offset in Kafka?
An offset is a unique, sequential integer assigned to each message within a partition. It’s Kafka’s way of tracking position.

Offsets are per partition, not global
Consumers commit their offset to track what they’ve processed
You can replay messages by resetting offsets to an earlier position
This is what makes Kafka fundamentally different from traditional queues — messages aren’t deleted on consumption; the consumer’s position is just a pointer.

5. What is a Consumer Group?
A Consumer Group is a set of consumers that jointly consume a topic. Kafka distributes partitions among group members:

Each partition is assigned to exactly one consumer within a group
Multiple groups can independently consume the same topic (fan-out pattern)
If you have more consumers than partitions, some consumers sit idle
Topic (4 partitions) + Consumer Group (2 consumers):
Consumer A → Partition 0, Partition 1
Consumer B → Partition 2, Partition 3
This is the core scalability primitive in Kafka.

⚙️ Section 2: Internals — Where Interviews Get Serious
6. How Does Kafka Maintain Message Ordering?
Kafka guarantees ordering within a partition, not across partitions.

Use a message key to route related messages to the same partition (e.g., customerId as key)
All messages with the same key land on the same partition in the same order
If ordering across all messages matters, use a single partition (sacrifices throughput)
In practice, business ordering is usually scoped to an entity (e.g., all events for order #123), and using the entity ID as key solves it cleanly.

7. What is Replication Factor?
Replication Factor defines how many copies of each partition exist across brokers. A replication factor of 3 means:

1 Leader replica (handles all reads/writes)
2 Follower replicas (passively replicate from leader)
Rule of thumb: replication factor ≥ 3 in production. It survives broker failures without data loss.

8. Difference Between Leader and Follower Replicas?
Leader Follower Handles producer writes ✅ Yes ❌ No Handles consumer reads ✅ Yes ❌ No Replicates data N/A (source) ✅ From leader Becomes active on failure Already active Elected as new leader

All traffic goes through the leader. Followers exist purely for fault tolerance.

9. What is ISR (In-Sync Replica)?
ISR (In-Sync Replicas) is the set of replicas that are fully caught up with the leader. A replica falls out of ISR if it falls too far behind (controlled by replica.lag.time.max.ms).

Why it matters:

acks=all waits for all ISR replicas to acknowledge — this is your durability guarantee
If ISR shrinks to 1 (only the leader), you’re effectively unprotected
Monitor ISR shrinkage in production — it’s an early warning of broker stress
10. What Happens if a Kafka Broker Goes Down?
Controller broker detects the failure via ZooKeeper/KRaft heartbeat timeout
For each partition where the failed broker was leader, a new leader is elected from the ISR
Producers and consumers automatically reconnect and route to the new leader
The failed broker’s partitions are reassigned
If min.insync.replicas is set (e.g., 2) and ISR drops below that, producers with acks=all will get a NotEnoughReplicasException — by design, to prevent data loss.

📤 Section 3: Producer Deep Dive
11. What is Producer Acknowledgement (acks)?
acks controls how many broker acknowledgements the producer requires before considering a write successful. It's the durability vs. latency tradeoff knob.

12. Difference Between acks=0, acks=1, and acks=all?
acks Behavior Risk Use Case 0 Fire and forget, no wait Data loss possible Metrics, logs (loss acceptable) 1 Leader acknowledges, followers may not have it Data loss if leader crashes before replication Moderate durability needs all (or -1) All ISR replicas must acknowledge Highest latency, no data loss Financial transactions, critical events

In production, acks=all + min.insync.replicas=2 is the gold standard for durability.

13. What is an Idempotent Producer?
An Idempotent Producer ensures that even if a message is sent multiple times (due to retries on network failures), Kafka deduplicates it — the broker stores exactly one copy.

Enable it with:

props.put(ProducerConfig.ENABLE_IDEMPOTENCE_CONFIG, true);
Kafka assigns each producer a PID (Producer ID) and a sequence number per partition. If a duplicate arrives, the broker discards it silently. This is prerequisite for Exactly Once Semantics.

14. How Does Kafka Handle Duplicate Messages?
Three layers of defense:

Idempotent Producer — deduplicates at the broker level during retries
Transactional API — atomic writes across multiple partitions/topics
Idempotent Consumer logic — deduplicate by tracking processed message IDs (e.g., in Redis or DB) for cross-system safety
Always assume your consumer might see duplicates and design it to be idempotent (processing the same message twice produces the same result).

📥 Section 4: Consumer Mechanics
15. What is Consumer Rebalancing?
Rebalancing is the process of redistributing partitions among consumers in a group. It’s triggered when:

A consumer joins or leaves
A consumer crashes or stops sending heartbeats
Topic partitions are added
During rebalancing, all consumption stops — this is called a “stop the world” event. It can cause significant lag spikes in production.

Mitigation: Use static group membership (group.instance.id) to avoid rebalances for temporary consumer restarts.

16. What Happens When a New Consumer Joins a Consumer Group?
New consumer sends a JoinGroup request to the group coordinator broker
Coordinator triggers a rebalance
One consumer is elected group leader
Group leader assigns partitions to all members (using an assignor strategy — RangeAssignor, RoundRobinAssignor, StickyAssignor)
Assignment is sent back via SyncGroup response
Consumption resumes
StickyAssignor is preferred in production — it minimizes partition movement during rebalances, reducing lag spikes.

17. What is Auto Offset Reset?
auto.offset.reset determines where a consumer starts reading when there's no committed offset (new consumer group, or offsets expired):

earliest — start from the very beginning of the topic
latest — start from new messages only (skip history)
none — throw an exception if no offset found
18. Difference Between earliest and latest Offset Reset?
Topic messages: [msg1, msg2, msg3, msg4] ← msg5 arrives now
earliest → Consumer starts at msg1 (reads all history)
latest   → Consumer starts at msg5 (only future messages)
Use earliest for event sourcing or replay scenarios. Use latest for real-time feeds where historical data is irrelevant.

🔁 Section 5: Failure Handling & Retry Mechanisms
19. What is a Dead Letter Queue (DLQ)?
A Dead Letter Queue is a separate Kafka topic where messages that repeatedly fail processing are routed. Instead of blocking the main consumer or silently dropping failures, DLQ gives you:

Visibility into what failed and why (store the exception with the message)
Non-blocking processing — bad messages don’t halt healthy ones
Reprocessing capability — fix the bug, replay from DLQ
orders-topic → Consumer → ❌ Fails 3 times → orders-topic.DLQ
In Spring Kafka, @KafkaListener with DeadLetterPublishingRecoverer handles this out of the box.

20. How Do You Retry Failed Kafka Messages?
Spring Kafka retry strategy:

@Bean
public DefaultErrorHandler errorHandler(KafkaTemplate<?, ?> template) {
    var recoverer = new DeadLetterPublishingRecoverer(template);
    var backoff = new ExponentialBackOffWithMaxRetries(3);
    backoff.setInitialInterval(1000L);
    backoff.setMultiplier(2.0);
    return new DefaultErrorHandler(recoverer, backoff);
}
Retry patterns:

Immediate retry — for transient errors (brief network blip)
Exponential backoff — for downstream service issues
Retry topic pattern — route to orders-retry-1, orders-retry-2 topics with delays
DLQ — after all retries exhausted
Never retry indefinitely — always set a max retry count and route to DLQ.

