22. What is Consumer Lag?
Consumer Lag is the difference between the latest offset (end of partition) and the consumer’s committed offset. It tells you how far behind a consumer is.

Latest Offset: 10,000
Consumer Offset: 9,500
Lag: 500 messages
High lag means your consumer can’t keep up with the producer rate — a production alert you should always have configured.

23. How Do You Debug Kafka Lag Issues?
Step-by-step approach:

Measure lag — kafka-consumer-groups.sh --describe or Grafana/Prometheus
Identify the slow partition — lag is usually uneven across partitions
Check consumer logs — long GC pauses? Slow DB calls inside onMessage?
Check max.poll.records — if set too high, processing takes too long → triggers rebalance
Scale consumers — add instances up to partition count
Profile the processing logic — is there an N+1 query or blocking HTTP call inside the listener?
🗄️ Section 6: Storage & Retention
24. What is Kafka Retention Policy?
Kafka retains messages based on your configuration — not based on consumption. Options:

log.retention.hours — time-based (default: 7 days)
log.retention.bytes — size-based per partition
Both — whichever limit is hit first
This enables message replay — a subscriber can re-read historical events simply by resetting their offset.

25. Difference Between Log Compaction and Retention?
