🔒 Section 7: Reliability & Guarantees
26. How Does Kafka Achieve Fault Tolerance?
Replication — every partition has N replicas across brokers
Leader election — automatic failover when a broker dies
ISR tracking — only caught-up replicas can become leaders
Durable storage — messages written to disk (not just memory)
Producer retries + idempotence — no message loss on transient failures
Consumer offset management — consumers can resume from last committed offset after restart
Kafka is designed such that a single broker failure is transparent to producers and consumers with proper configuration.

27. How Does Partitioning Improve Scalability?
Partitioning is Kafka’s horizontal scaling mechanism:

Write parallelism — multiple producers write to different partitions simultaneously
Read parallelism — each partition is consumed by one consumer thread (within a group)
Distribution — partitions spread across brokers, no single bottleneck
Throughput formula (roughly):

Effective throughput = Single partition throughput × Number of partitions
A topic with 12 partitions, each handling 50MB/s, gives you ~600MB/s aggregate throughput.

31. What is Exactly Once Semantics in Kafka?
Exactly Once Semantics (EOS) guarantees that each message is processed exactly one time — no duplicates, no losses. Achieved by combining:

Idempotent Producer (dedup at broker)
Kafka Transactions (atomic multi-partition writes)
read-process-write in a single transaction (consumer offset + output committed atomically)
producer.initTransactions();
producer.beginTransaction();
producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("output-topic", result));
producer.sendOffsetsToTransaction(offsets, consumerGroupMetadata);
producer.commitTransaction();
EOS adds ~10–20% latency overhead. Use only where it’s truly required (financial systems, billing).

32. At Most Once vs At Least Once vs Exactly Once
Semantic How Risk Use Case At Most Once Commit offset before processing Message loss on crash Metrics, telemetry At Least Once Commit offset after processing Duplicate processing possible Most business events (with idempotent consumers) Exactly Once Transactional API Performance cost Payments, inventory, billing

In practice, 90% of systems use At Least Once with idempotent consumer logic — it’s simpler and fast enough.

🌊 Section 8: Kafka Streams
33. What is Kafka Streams?
Kafka Streams is a client-side Java library for building stream processing applications. No separate cluster needed — it runs inside your application.

StreamsBuilder builder = new StreamsBuilder();
builder.stream("orders")
       .filter((key, order) -> order.getAmount() > 1000)
       .to("high-value-orders");
Features: stateful operations, windowing, joins, aggregations, fault-tolerant state stores (backed by Kafka topics).

34. Difference Between Kafka Streams and Spark Streaming?
Kafka Streams Spark Streaming Processing model Record-by-record (true streaming) Micro-batch Deployment Embedded in your app Separate cluster Latency Milliseconds Seconds (micro-batch) Complexity Low (just a library) High (cluster management) State management Local RocksDB + Kafka Spark RDD/DataFrame Best for Lightweight per-service processing Heavy analytics, ML pipelines

Use Kafka Streams when you want stream processing without the overhead of a separate cluster.

🏭 Section 9: Spring Boot Integration
28. What is KafkaTemplate in Spring Boot?
KafkaTemplate is Spring Kafka's high-level producer abstraction. It wraps the native KafkaProducer and integrates with Spring's transaction support.

@Autowired
private KafkaTemplate<String, OrderEvent> kafkaTemplate;
public void publishOrder(Order order) {
    kafkaTemplate.send("orders", order.getId(), new OrderEvent(order))
                 .whenComplete((result, ex) -> {
                     if (ex != null) log.error("Failed to send", ex);
                     else log.info("Sent to partition {}", result.getRecordMetadata().partition());
                 });
}
29. What is @KafkaListener?
@KafkaListener is the consumer-side annotation that registers a method as a Kafka message handler:

@KafkaListener(
    topics = "orders",
    groupId = "order-service",
    containerFactory = "kafkaListenerContainerFactory"
)
public void handleOrder(ConsumerRecord<String, OrderEvent> record) {
    log.info("Processing order {} from partition {}",
             record.key(), record.partition());
    orderService.process(record.value());
}
It supports batch listening, manual offset acknowledgment, and error handling through the container factory configuration.

30. How Do You Handle Kafka Consumer Exceptions in Spring Boot?
@Bean
public ConcurrentKafkaListenerContainerFactory<String, Object> factory(
        ConsumerFactory<String, Object> cf,
        KafkaTemplate<String, Object> template) {
    var factory = new ConcurrentKafkaListenerContainerFactory<String, Object>();
    factory.setConsumerFactory(cf);
    // DLQ recoverer: failed messages → topic.DLQ
    var recoverer = new DeadLetterPublishingRecoverer(template);
    // Retry 3 times with exponential backoff, then send to DLQ
    var backoff = new ExponentialBackOffWithMaxRetries(3);
    backoff.setInitialInterval(500L);
    backoff.setMultiplier(2.0);
    factory.setCommonErrorHandler(new DefaultErrorHandler(recoverer, backoff));
    return factory;
}
Key decisions:

Distinguish retriable errors (network, DB timeout) from non-retriable (deserialization failure, validation error)
Never retry non-retriable errors — they’ll always fail; send straight to DLQ
Always include exception metadata in DLQ message headers for observability
