36. How Do You Monitor Kafka in Production?
Essential metrics:

Consumer metrics:
- records-lag-max (per partition)
- records-consumed-rate
- commit-rate
Producer metrics:
- record-error-rate
- request-latency-avg
Broker metrics:
- UnderReplicatedPartitions (must be 0)
- ISR shrink/expand rate
- BytesIn/BytesOut rate
- ActiveControllerCount (must be 1)
Stack: JMX → Prometheus JMX Exporter → Grafana dashboards
Managed options: Confluent Control Center, Datadog Kafka integration, AWS MSK metrics

Non-negotiable alerts:

UnderReplicatedPartitions > 0
Consumer lag > threshold for N minutes
ActiveControllerCount != 1
37. What Happens If Partition Count is Increased Later?
Increasing partitions is a one-way, irreversible operation in Kafka.

What changes:

New messages are distributed across more partitions
Message ordering by key breaks — the same key may now go to a different partition
Existing consumers rebalance to cover new partitions
Historical messages in old partitions remain there
Best practice: Design for the right partition count upfront. If you must scale, use a migration strategy — create a new topic with the correct partition count and migrate producers/consumers.

38. Can Kafka Guarantee Message Ordering Across Partitions?
No. Kafka only guarantees ordering within a single partition.

If your business logic requires strict global ordering:

Use a single partition (no parallelism — serious throughput trade-off)
Or use an application-level ordering layer (e.g., sequence numbers + reordering buffer)
Or rethink your design — true global ordering needs are rare; usually per-entity ordering suffices
39. What is the Ideal Partition Strategy?
Key guidelines:

Partition count — max(target throughput / per-partition throughput, expected consumer count). Start with 3× the expected broker count.
Key selection — high-cardinality keys that distribute evenly (avoid using boolean fields or low-cardinality enums as keys)
Replication factor — minimum 3 in production
Over-partition slightly — easier to scale consumers; can’t reduce partitions later
Avoid too many partitions — each partition has a file handle and memory overhead; 10,000+ partitions per broker causes GC pressure
40. How Does Kafka Work Internally?
The full picture:

Producer
  └─→ Serializes message
  └─→ Determines partition (key hash / round-robin / custom)
  └─→ Batches messages in RecordAccumulator
  └─→ Sender thread flushes to Leader Broker
Broker (Leader)
  └─→ Writes to partition log (segment files on disk)
  └─→ Follower brokers pull and replicate
  └─→ Returns ack to producer (based on acks config)
Consumer
  └─→ Polls broker (fetch request with offset)
  └─→ Broker returns batch of records
  └─→ Consumer processes records
  └─→ Commits offset (auto or manual)
Storage internals:

Each partition is a series of segment files (1GB default)
Messages are appended sequentially — O(1) write performance
Zero-copy transfer (Linux sendfile) for reads — data goes directly from disk to network buffer
Index files map offsets to file positions for O(log n) random access
This sequential I/O design is why Kafka can sustain millions of messages per second on commodity hardware.

